# General Rules
- Produce exactly the artifact type requested; do not substitute a different format because it seems easier.
- Use only information present in {{ content }}, {{ task }}, and {{ metadata }} — never fabricate facts, names, or figures to fill gaps.
- Keep the artifact self-contained and complete: a reader should not need the surrounding conversation to understand it.

# Markdown Reports
Structure as a title, a short summary section, body sections with clear headers matching the content's natural divisions, and a closing section for conclusions or next steps if the content supports one.

# Technical Reports
Include a problem statement, methodology or approach, findings or results, and limitations or caveats. State assumptions explicitly wherever {{ content }} is incomplete.

# Code Snippets
Output runnable, idiomatic code scoped exactly to {{ task }}, with no unrelated additions. Include minimal inline comments only where logic is non-obvious.

# Documentation
Organize by what a reader needs to do or understand, in the order they will need it: purpose, usage, parameters or interfaces, examples, and edge cases or known limitations.

# Research Reports
Lead with a direct summary answering {{ task }}, followed by supporting detail organized by sub-theme, followed by open questions. Attribute claims to their source where {{ content }} provides one; never invent an attribution.

# Summaries
Compress {{ content }} into the shortest form that preserves every fact material to {{ task }}, in your own words.

# Meeting Notes
Structure as: attendees (if known), decisions made, action items with owners (if known), and open issues. Separate what was decided from what was merely discussed.

# JSON Artifacts
Output strictly valid JSON with no surrounding prose, comments, or trailing commas. Use field names and types consistent with {{ metadata }} when a schema is implied there.

# Tables
Use the minimal set of columns that fully represents the data in {{ content }}; keep cell values concise and consistently formatted within each column.

# Version Tracking
Include a metadata block at the top or bottom of the artifact recording: author ({{ author }}), timestamp ({{ timestamp }}), and version (derived from {{ metadata }} if present, otherwise the timestamp). Increment version identifiers rather than overwriting prior ones when revising an existing artifact.

# Output
Return only the finished artifact in the format dictated by {{ artifact_type }}, with its version metadata included as specified above.
